## 5. Hyperparameters Explained

| Parameter | Default | What It Controls |
|---|---|---|
| `n_estimators` | 100 | Number of trees. More = better, but slower |
| `max_depth` | None | Max depth of each tree. None = fully grown |
| `max_features` | `"auto"` | Features per split. `"auto"` = n_features/3 for regression |
| `min_samples_split` | 2 | Min samples required to split an internal node |
| `min_samples_leaf` | 1 | Min samples required at a leaf node |
| `bootstrap` | True | Whether to use bootstrap samples |
| `oob_score` | False | Use OOB samples to estimate generalization score |
| `random_state` | None | Seed for reproducibility |
| `n_jobs` | None | Parallel jobs. `-1` = use all CPU cores |

### Tuning Tips
- Start with `n_estimators=100`, increase if score improves
- Use `max_depth=10–20` to prevent overfitting on noisy data
- Use `GridSearchCV` or `RandomizedSearchCV` for tuning

---

## 6. Advantages & Disadvantages

### ✅ Advantages
- **High accuracy** — usually outperforms single decision trees
- **Handles missing values** — can impute or work around them
- **Robust to outliers** — averaging smooths extreme predictions
- **No feature scaling needed** — tree-based, not distance-based
- **Built-in feature importance** — free interpretability
- **OOB score** — free validation without a separate validation set
- **Handles high-dimensional data** well

### ❌ Disadvantages
- **Slow to predict** — must traverse all trees
- **Memory intensive** — stores all N trees
- **Black-box** — harder to interpret than a single tree
- **Not great for extrapolation** — can't predict beyond training data range
- **Biased toward features with more levels** — in some implementations

